And in Persian, there are some words that kind of became the English words checkmate that mean the king is captured or the king is defeated.

244
00:12:46,640 --> 00:12:48,540
Okay, so that's what it means.

245
00:12:48,920 --> 00:12:50,160
It doesn't mean the king is dead.

246
00:12:50,640 --> 00:12:53,340
No, it doesn't mean that he's dead, it means that he's been taken.

247
00:12:53,700 --> 00:13:02,500
And so over time, the Persian word went into Arabic, and Arabic became French and English, and then now we say checkmate.

248
00:13:02,780 --> 00:13:03,900
Checkmate, okay, interesting.

249
00:13:04,720 --> 00:13:11,640
This is a very interesting word, and I'm sure everybody that's listening has a different translation for it in their own language.

250
00:13:11,640 --> 00:13:15,640
I know, for example, that in Spanish, we would say jaque mate.

251
00:13:15,640 --> 00:13:17,640
Jaque mate.

252
00:13:17,640 --> 00:13:23,640
Right, so I guess like jaque is something's in check, it's in danger, and then mate, kind of like kill.

253
00:13:23,640 --> 00:13:29,950
So maybe it's probably not translated properly, because as you mentioned, it should mean defeated or captured, not killed.

